(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["splanchnic"]="<h1>Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-beds\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> An unusual route through two capillary beds</h2>\n<p>The gut is supplied by three unpaired arteries from the aorta: the <strong><span data-term=\"coeliac-trunk\">coeliac trunk</span></strong>, the <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> and the <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The unusual part is where the blood goes next.</strong> Venous blood from the gut and spleen does <strong>not</strong> go straight back to the heart. It collects in the <strong><span data-term=\"portal-vein\">portal vein</span></strong> (formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins) and passes through the <strong>liver first</strong>.</p>\n<p>So this blood crosses <strong>two capillary beds in a row</strong>: the gut's capillaries, then the liver's <strong><span data-term=\"sinusoid\">sinusoids</span></strong>. <strong>Why?</strong> Everything absorbed from the gut (nutrients, drugs, toxins, bacteria) is sent to the liver to be processed <strong>before</strong> it reaches the rest of the body.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portal_venous_system-en.svg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portal_venous_system-en.svg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portal_venous_system-en.svg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portal_venous_system-en.svg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Labelled anatomical illustration of portal venous return from abdominal digestive organs to the liver\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Portal venous system.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portal_venous_system-en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-the-liver-s-double-blood-supply-and-its-zones\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> The liver's double blood supply and its zones</h2>\n<p>The liver gets blood from <strong>two inflows</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Inflow</th>\n  <th>Share of the flow</th>\n  <th>Brings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Portal vein</strong></td>\n  <td>About <strong>75%</strong></td>\n  <td>Nutrient-rich, but partly deoxygenated, blood from the gut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Hepatic artery</strong></td>\n  <td>About <strong>25%</strong></td>\n  <td>Oxygen-rich arterial blood (about half the liver's oxygen)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both enter at the <strong><span data-term=\"portal-triad\">portal triad</span></strong> (portal vein branch + hepatic artery branch + bile duct). They <strong>mix in the sinusoids</strong>, the leaky, wide liver capillaries, and flow towards the <strong>central vein</strong> → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava. <strong>Bile flows the opposite way</strong>, towards the bile ducts in the triad.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_scheme1.jpg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_scheme1.jpg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_scheme1.jpg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_scheme1.jpg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Illustration of hepatic lobule organization, central vein, portal triads and acinar zones with oxygen delivery gradients\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Hepatic lobule and acinar oxygen zonation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">MarquardtM, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_scheme1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Detailed labelled anatomical illustration of liver vessels, portal tract and hepatic lobule structures\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Liver vascular and lobular anatomy.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Gwilz, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Zones: why the centre suffers first.</strong> Along each sinusoid, liver cells take oxygen out as blood passes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zone 1</strong> (near the portal triad) gets the <strong>freshest, most oxygen-rich</strong> blood.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"hepatic-zones\">Zone 3</span></strong> (near the central vein) gets blood that has <strong>already given up much of its oxygen</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So when blood flow or oxygen falls (in shock or heart failure), <strong>zone 3 is damaged first</strong>.</p>\n<p>Between the sinusoid lining and the liver cells is a narrow gap, the <strong><span data-term=\"space-of-disse\">space of Disse</span></strong>, where plasma touches the cells directly. Fluid that collects here drains as hepatic lymph.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Blood flow after meals, and in shock</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>After a meal</strong>, the gut works harder and its arterioles dilate, so splanchnic blood flow rises: <strong><span data-term=\"postprandial-hyperaemia\">postprandial hyperaemia</span></strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The splanchnic veins are a blood reservoir</strong>: stretchy, and holding a large volume.</li>\n<li><strong>In haemorrhage</strong>, sympathetic nerves <strong>constrict</strong> the splanchnic vessels. This sends stored blood back to the heart and diverts flow to the brain and heart. It helps hold up blood pressure, but if it lasts, the <strong>gut lining becomes ischaemic</strong> and can break down. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does gut venous blood pass through the liver before reaching the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The liver adds oxygen to the venous blood from the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The portal vein is too narrow to reach the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The liver processes absorbed nutrients and toxins first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The gut veins have no valves, so they need the liver to pump</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The portal route sends everything absorbed from the gut to the liver to be processed before it enters the general circulation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In shock, why are liver cells near the central vein (zone 3) damaged first?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They receive blood only from the portal vein</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They are closest to the hepatic artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They do not have sinusoids</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They receive blood after upstream cells have used much of its oxygen</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Blood flows from the portal triad to the central vein, losing oxygen along the way, so zone 3 runs short first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> How do the splanchnic vessels help in haemorrhage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They dilate to receive more blood from the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They constrict, sending stored blood back to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They make new red cells to replace lost blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They stop the liver from releasing glucose</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Sympathetic constriction squeezes the splanchnic reservoir and diverts flow to vital organs, at the cost of gut perfusion.</p></details></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-does-fluid-collect-as-ascites\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Why does fluid collect as ascites?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"ascites\">Ascites</span></strong> is fluid in the peritoneal cavity. It usually develops when the liver is scarred (<strong><span data-term=\"cirrhosis\">cirrhosis</span></strong>), through several routes at once:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>High portal pressure:</strong> scarring blocks flow through the liver, so pressure backs up in the portal vein and sinusoids, pushing fluid out. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Much more hepatic lymph:</strong> the leaky sinusoids filter so much fluid that the lymphatics can't keep up, and the excess weeps into the abdomen.</li>\n<li><strong>The kidneys keep salt and water</strong>, sensing underfilled arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Low albumin</strong> (a failing liver makes less), so the pull holding fluid in the vessels weakens. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §5</a>)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Right heart failure</strong> can cause ascites too: high pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver.</p>\n<p>The protein content of ascitic fluid <strong>varies with the cause</strong>: it is not always high, and not always low.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which change most directly starts ascites in cirrhosis?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised pressure in the hepatic artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised pressure in the portal vein and liver sinusoids</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Increased flow through the coeliac trunk</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Increased albumin production by the liver</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Scarring obstructs flow, raising portal and sinusoidal pressure. Lymph overflow, salt retention and low albumin add to it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How can right heart failure cause ascites?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises albumin, pulling fluid into the abdomen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It blocks the portal vein directly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It stops the kidneys from retaining salt</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> High right atrial pressure is passed back into the hepatic veins and sinusoids, so filtration rises.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do the liver's sinusoids produce so much lymph?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They have no blood flow at all</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They contain muscle that pumps fluid out</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They are very leaky, so fluid and protein filter out easily</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They are lined by thick, continuous capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries, so much fluid passes into the space of Disse and drains as hepatic lymph.</p></details></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Gut arteries:</strong> coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric.</li>\n<li><strong>Portal route:</strong> gut capillaries → portal vein → <strong>liver sinusoids</strong> → hepatic veins → IVC: two capillary beds in a row.</li>\n<li><strong>Two liver inflows:</strong> the portal vein (about 75% of flow) and the hepatic artery (about 25%, but about half the oxygen).</li>\n<li><strong>Zone 1</strong> (portal triad) is oxygen-rich; <strong>zone 3</strong> (central vein) is oxygen-poor and damaged first.</li>\n<li><strong>After meals</strong>, gut flow rises. <strong>In haemorrhage</strong>, splanchnic vessels constrict, mobilising blood but risking gut ischaemia.</li>\n<li><strong>Ascites:</strong> portal hypertension + lymph overflow + salt retention + low albumin (cirrhosis), or right heart failure.</li>\n</ol>\n";
